## The residence permit for retirees

Mauritius offers a residence permit to retirees aged **50 or older**. The Economic Development Board's conditions:

- A bank statement from your home country showing at least **USD 24 000** or a guaranteed income of **USD 2 000 a month**.
- Once the permit is issued make an initial transfer of at least USD 2 000 to a Mauritian bank within 60 days. After that transfer USD 24 000 a year or USD 2 000 a month.
- The permit is valid for **ten years** and renewable for ten more. The fee is USD 1 000 and since 1 December 2025 there is also a non refundable application fee of USD 50.
- Your spouse and children and parents who depend on you can apply for a permit for the same period.

You may invest in any business as long as you are not employed in it and take no salary from it.

## Permanent residence after five years

After five years as a retired resident and with at least **USD 200 000** transferred in total over those five years you can apply for a **Permanent Residence Permit**. It is valid for twenty years and renewable.

## Applying

The application starts online on the EDB's licensing platform. You do not need to be in Mauritius until the approval in principle. Among other things you will need your birth certificate and passport and a certificate of character covering ten years and recent photographs. Later you will need a Mauritian bank statement and a medical certificate.

## Buying a home

Retirees can buy under the [PDS](/en/living-in-the-west/pds) or [IRS](/en/living-in-the-west/irs) or [RES](/en/living-in-the-west/res) or [Smart City](/en/living-in-the-west/smart-city) schemes or an apartment in a building of at least ground plus two floors. A home of USD 375 000 or more gives its own residence permit. To try a long stay before buying look at the one year renewable [Premium Visa](https://residency.mu/live/mauritius-premium-visa/). It is made for stays of up to a year without working in Mauritius.

## Tax

You become tax resident if you stay 183 days or more in a year or 270 days over the current and previous year. The EDB notes that foreign income (including pensions) is taxable for residents. How a pension is treated depends on the tax treaty with your country and on whether it is brought into Mauritius. Take advice before you move.

## Where to retire on the west coast

- **[Flic en Flac](/en/areas/flic-en-flac)**: one of the longest beaches on the island. Lively at weekends and relaxed the rest of the week.
- **[Tamarin](/en/areas/tamarin)**: a village on a wide bay ringed by mountains with dolphins in the early morning.
- **[Black River](/en/areas/black-river)**: the working heart of the coast with La Preneuse beach and the national park on the doorstep.
- **[La Gaulette](/en/areas/la-gaulette)**: a small unhurried village between the mountains and a wide lagoon.
- **[Le Morne](/en/areas/le-morne)**: some of the finest beaches on the island below the UNESCO listed mountain.
- **[Chamarel](/en/areas/chamarel)**: a village on a wooded plateau about 260 metres up with cooler air.

Each area page has a Living section with what daily life is like.

*Rules and amounts change. Everything here was checked against the Economic Development Board's own pages on 2 October 2026. Confirm the details with the EDB and your notary and the developer before you sign.*
